  "content": {
    "id": "p140-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Consider $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}$ and $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}$. Find the domain and range of the following.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p140-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$g(f(x))$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Domain: $(1,\\infty)$; range: $(0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}g(f(x))&=g\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{x-1}\\right)\\\\g(f(x))&=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x-1}}\\end{align*}$$\nThe inner function requires $x\\ne1$, and the square root requires $\\frac{1}{x-1}\\ge0$. Since the numerator is positive, these conditions hold exactly when $x>1$.\n\nDomain: $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nOn this domain, $f(x)$ takes every value in $(0,\\infty)$. Taking square roots gives every positive value, but never zero.\n\nRange: $(0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p140-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(g(x))$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p140-q11-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Domain: ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "[0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "; range: ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(-\\infty,-1]\\cup(0,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p140-q11-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Require ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x\\ge0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x\\ne1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then take reciprocals of the allowed denominator values.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=f(\\sqrt{x})\\\\f(g(x))&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}-1}\\end{align*}$$\nThe inner square root requires $x\\ge0$. The outer reciprocal requires $\\sqrt{x}\\ne1$, so $x\\ne1$.\n\nDomain: $[0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$.\n\nOn this domain, $\\sqrt{x}$ takes every value in $[0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$. Hence $\\sqrt{x}-1$ takes every value in $[-1,0)\\cup(0,\\infty)$.\n\nThe reciprocals of values in $[-1,0)$ fill $(-\\infty,-1]$. The reciprocals of positive values fill $(0,\\infty)$.\n\nRange: $(-\\infty,-1]\\cup(0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
